1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,100
The new Jace precon isn't even out yet,
and people have already found a way to win

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:05,000
the game with it.

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,420
With one card, straight out of the box.

4
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:08,620
Yeah.

5
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:09,658
Wizards messed up.

6
00:00:09,678 --> 00:00:13,138
Hello everyone, this is Wil
D. Card with Wild Card Commander.

7
00:00:13,518 --> 00:00:16,258
Today we're looking at
the Dack and Venser combo.

8
00:00:16,538 --> 00:00:22,298
A Redditor called Huaojozu posted it on
r/magicTCG, and the thread's been going

9
00:00:22,318 --> 00:00:23,338
nuts ever since.

10
00:00:23,718 --> 00:00:26,698
We'll go through how it works
and why it's even legal.

11
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:31,338
And there's one card in this same deck
that stops you from winning with it.

12
00:00:31,398 --> 00:00:32,398
We'll get to that.

13
00:00:32,478 --> 00:00:33,443
Let's take a look.

14
00:00:33,463 --> 00:00:34,553
All right, real quick.

15
00:00:34,953 --> 00:00:37,173
The deck is Multiverse Reforged.

16
00:00:37,313 --> 00:00:40,593
It's the four-color Jace precon from
Reality Fracture, and it's out October

17
00:00:40,613 --> 00:00:43,573
2nd. Two of the new cards
in it are the problem.

18
00:00:43,813 --> 00:00:46,113
First up, Dack Fayden, Helping Hand.

19
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:48,553
Dack's a six-mana creature now.

20
00:00:48,573 --> 00:00:49,613
Already a bit weird.

21
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:53,673
When he enters, you reveal cards off the
top until you hit one creature for each

22
00:00:53,693 --> 00:00:56,913
opponent. In a normal four-player
game, that's three creatures.

23
00:00:57,113 --> 00:01:00,133
They come in under your control, you
shuffle, and they're goaded for the rest

24
00:01:00,153 --> 00:01:01,113
of the game.

25
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:02,993
Then you hand one to each opponent.

26
00:01:03,853 --> 00:01:04,853
Merry Christmas.

27
00:01:05,353 --> 00:01:07,593
Next up, Venser, Fervent Forger.

28
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:11,753
When Venser enters, one of his modes makes
two token copies of target permanent an

29
00:01:11,773 --> 00:01:12,833
opponent controls.

30
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:15,853
They've got haste, and you
sacrifice them at the next end step.

31
00:01:16,018 --> 00:01:18,638
Now let's say we flip Venser
as one of those three.

32
00:01:18,658 --> 00:01:20,178
Dack's ability is resolving.

33
00:01:20,198 --> 00:01:24,218
Venser comes in under your control,
so when his trigger happens, it's your

34
00:01:24,238 --> 00:01:28,078
trigger. Then, still in the middle of
Dack's ability, Venser gets handed to an

35
00:01:28,098 --> 00:01:29,918
opponent. Dack finishes.

36
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:32,318
Now we put Venser's trigger on the stack.

37
00:01:32,818 --> 00:01:34,798
And Venser's on an
opponent's side now, right?

38
00:01:34,858 --> 00:01:35,718
They control him.

39
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:38,518
So you can point his ability at… Venser.

40
00:01:39,158 --> 00:01:40,158
Yeah. Himself.

41
00:01:41,078 --> 00:01:42,808
We get two token Vensers.

42
00:01:43,018 --> 00:01:45,038
Legend rule, you keep one, and
the other goes to the graveyard.

43
00:01:45,058 --> 00:01:46,878
But they both entered.

44
00:01:47,098 --> 00:01:49,298
That means we've got
two new Venser triggers.

45
00:01:49,698 --> 00:01:53,278
One targets the original Venser
again, and that keeps the loop going.

46
00:01:53,968 --> 00:01:56,268
The other can target anything
an opponent controls.

47
00:01:56,908 --> 00:01:58,768
Remember the other two
creatures Dack flipped?

48
00:01:58,868 --> 00:02:00,628
They're on your opponents' boards now.

49
00:02:00,848 --> 00:02:02,608
Our second trigger can copy one of those.

50
00:02:02,968 --> 00:02:04,368
And it just keeps going.

51
00:02:04,648 --> 00:02:07,988
Every loop, you get two hasty
copies of whatever you pointed at.

52
00:02:08,088 --> 00:02:10,988
Their creatures, their
artifacts, even their lands.

53
00:02:11,528 --> 00:02:14,188
Copy the lands, and that's
basically unlimited mana.

54
00:02:14,608 --> 00:02:18,368
Loop it as many times as you want, and you
end up with endless copies of their whole

55
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:19,388
board.

56
00:02:19,898 --> 00:02:22,518
They all get sacrificed at the
beginning of the next end step, though.

57
00:02:22,848 --> 00:02:24,888
So do it before combat, then swing.

58
00:02:25,568 --> 00:02:27,168
And yeah, before anyone types it.

59
00:02:27,228 --> 00:02:28,288
It's two cards.

60
00:02:28,748 --> 00:02:30,188
But you only cast one.

61
00:02:30,208 --> 00:02:32,488
Dack goes and finds the other one for you.

62
00:02:32,646 --> 00:02:33,286
Quick thing.

63
00:02:33,306 --> 00:02:36,466
I break down weird new interactions
like this before they hit your table.

64
00:02:36,906 --> 00:02:38,426
Subscribe, or Dack goads your commander.

65
00:02:38,946 --> 00:02:40,146
Okay. Quick quiz.

66
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:42,786
When does Venser's trigger
actually pick its target?

67
00:02:43,146 --> 00:02:44,146
Pause if you want.

68
00:02:44,306 --> 00:02:46,586
Three, two, one.

69
00:02:46,728 --> 00:02:48,348
It's when it goes on the stack.

70
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:51,388
That's the thing everyone in the
Reddit thread kept asking about.

71
00:02:51,708 --> 00:02:54,248
Venser's ability triggers
the moment he enters.

72
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:56,228
But it doesn't go on the stack yet.

73
00:02:56,348 --> 00:02:59,788
It waits until the next time a player
would get priority, which is after Dack's

74
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:01,248
whole ability is done.

75
00:03:01,388 --> 00:03:03,388
That's rule 603.3.

76
00:03:03,828 --> 00:03:07,268
And you choose targets as it goes
on the stack, not when it triggers.

77
00:03:07,408 --> 00:03:09,479
That's 603.3d.

78
00:03:09,928 --> 00:03:13,088
The trigger's yours, because you
controlled Venser when it triggered.

79
00:03:13,108 --> 00:03:14,488
That's 603.3a.

80
00:03:14,708 --> 00:03:18,308
But you pick the target after
he's already switched sides.

81
00:03:18,588 --> 00:03:20,048
I mean, it's a tiny gap.

82
00:03:20,068 --> 00:03:21,888
But yeah, that's the
whole reason it works.

83
00:03:22,157 --> 00:03:24,437
Okay, so how often does this happen?

84
00:03:24,717 --> 00:03:26,597
We've got 18 creatures in this deck.

85
00:03:26,997 --> 00:03:29,117
Dack's one of them, and he's
already on the battlefield.

86
00:03:29,277 --> 00:03:30,717
That leaves 17.

87
00:03:31,117 --> 00:03:33,117
Dack grabs the first three he finds.

88
00:03:33,497 --> 00:03:37,197
Assuming you haven't drawn any other
creatures yet, the chance Venser's one of

89
00:03:37,217 --> 00:03:38,657
them is three in seventeen.

90
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:40,157
So, about 18 percent.

91
00:03:40,177 --> 00:03:41,697
But you can help it along.

92
00:03:41,717 --> 00:03:44,117
Say Venser's already in your hand.

93
00:03:44,457 --> 00:03:47,777
Brainstorm or Brainsurge, both in the
deck, let you put him back on top.

94
00:03:47,797 --> 00:03:50,057
Then Dack finds him every time.

95
00:03:50,637 --> 00:03:52,137
We can get there with Jace too.

96
00:03:52,337 --> 00:03:56,057
His minus three exiles one of your other
creatures, then puts the next creature or

97
00:03:56,077 --> 00:03:57,997
planeswalker off the top into play.

98
00:03:58,297 --> 00:03:59,677
That could be Dack.

99
00:04:00,277 --> 00:04:01,277
Lots of ifs.

100
00:04:01,497 --> 00:04:03,137
But it's all in the box.

101
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:04,440
Now, there's a catch.

102
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:06,400
It's my favorite part of this whole thing.

103
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:09,080
Every creature Dack finds
goes to a different opponent.

104
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,840
And this deck also has Darksteel Angel.

105
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,060
Darksteel Angel says you can't lose the
game, and your opponents can't win the

106
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:19,240
game. So if Dack flips Venser and the
Angel, the Angel goes to an opponent.

107
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:21,680
And now you're one of
the people who can't win.

108
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,380
You've got a board full of infinite
copies, and you're not allowed to win.

109
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:27,130
Love that for you.

110
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:29,990
And it's indestructible,
so killing it won't work.

111
00:04:30,310 --> 00:04:32,230
Luckily, this deck has
other ways to get rid of it.

112
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:33,250
Hold that thought.

113
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:35,690
The dream version is the other way around.

114
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:39,610
If Dack also finds Archon of Cruelty,
we don't even need to attack.

115
00:04:39,930 --> 00:04:42,530
Every copy of Archon makes
an opponent lose three life.

116
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:45,790
The problem is, every copy
also makes you draw a card.

117
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:49,330
Three opponents on 40
life is around 40 copies.

118
00:04:49,730 --> 00:04:51,190
Count your library before you start.

119
00:04:51,321 --> 00:04:53,441
So what if someone pulls this on you?

120
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:56,121
Every loop needs the original Venser.

121
00:04:56,281 --> 00:04:58,081
The one sitting on an opponent's board.

122
00:04:58,481 --> 00:05:01,921
If he leaves while a trigger's pointing
at him, that trigger does nothing.

123
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:04,818
That's rule 608.2b.

124
00:05:05,341 --> 00:05:06,781
Swords to Plowshares does it.

125
00:05:06,881 --> 00:05:08,411
Path to Exile, Despark,

126
00:05:08,741 --> 00:05:10,821
even a bounce spell like Fatehold Charm.

127
00:05:11,301 --> 00:05:13,241
All in this same precon.

128
00:05:13,481 --> 00:05:14,801
They handle that Angel too.

129
00:05:15,261 --> 00:05:17,181
Hold one up, and that's your moment.

130
00:05:17,423 --> 00:05:19,943
One more thing people in
the thread pointed out.

131
00:05:20,223 --> 00:05:22,183
This can happen to you, too.

132
00:05:22,643 --> 00:05:26,903
If you've got Venser out and an opponent
copies him, their copy's trigger can

133
00:05:26,923 --> 00:05:28,143
target your Venser.

134
00:05:28,163 --> 00:05:30,843
And now they're the one going infinite.

135
00:05:31,303 --> 00:05:34,023
This precon even has a
Cursed Mirror that can do it.

136
00:05:34,043 --> 00:05:36,303
Which makes Venser kind of a liability.

137
00:05:36,668 --> 00:05:38,728
So, did Wizards mess up?

138
00:05:38,948 --> 00:05:39,808
Yeah.

139
00:05:39,828 --> 00:05:43,788
Most precons land in Bracket 2, and
Bracket 2 isn't supposed to have two-card

140
00:05:43,808 --> 00:05:46,148
infinites. This one ships with one.

141
00:05:46,368 --> 00:05:47,748
Has Wizards said anything?

142
00:05:47,948 --> 00:05:49,408
Not yet, as far as I know.

143
00:05:49,448 --> 00:05:51,008
If they do, I'll pin it in the comments.

144
00:05:51,368 --> 00:05:52,808
Should we worry about it, though?

145
00:05:52,928 --> 00:05:53,688
Not really.

146
00:05:53,708 --> 00:05:56,828
Most of us won't ever see this
happen, and it takes a lot of luck.

147
00:05:56,948 --> 00:05:59,708
But if you know about it, tell
the table before you shuffle up.

148
00:05:59,728 --> 00:06:03,608
Something like, "hey, this deck can go
infinite by accident, you cool with that?"

149
00:06:03,788 --> 00:06:05,008
Some groups will love it.

150
00:06:05,028 --> 00:06:05,688
Some won't.

151
00:06:05,708 --> 00:06:06,708
So just ask.

152
00:06:07,048 --> 00:06:10,088
If you'd rather not deal with it,
cutting Venser takes two seconds.

153
00:06:10,348 --> 00:06:12,388
A lot of people in the
thread were cutting Dack.

154
00:06:12,408 --> 00:06:16,408
But someone made a good point: handing
everyone a random big creature is a pretty

155
00:06:16,428 --> 00:06:18,088
fun political game on its own.

156
00:06:18,108 --> 00:06:19,288
So I'd keep him.

157
00:06:19,488 --> 00:06:21,028
Would you let this fly at your table?

158
00:06:21,048 --> 00:06:23,268
Or is Venser coming out
before the first game?

159
00:06:23,408 --> 00:06:24,408
Tell me in the comments.

160
00:06:24,563 --> 00:06:25,723
So that's the combo.

161
00:06:25,743 --> 00:06:30,443
Dack hands Venser over, the trigger waits,
and by the time you pick a target, he's

162
00:06:30,463 --> 00:06:31,283
theirs.

163
00:06:31,303 --> 00:06:32,743
All from one box.

164
00:06:33,343 --> 00:06:36,043
Thread's linked below, so
go give Huaojozu some love.

165
00:06:36,223 --> 00:06:38,543
And if you want more breakdowns
like this, subscribe.

166
00:06:38,783 --> 00:06:40,743
Thanks for watching, and
I'll see you in the next one.
